  "note": "Re-judged 2026-10-24 (independent auditor, authored none of the change). RFC 7911 Section 2: \"A BGP speaker that re-advertises a route MUST generate its own Path Identifier to be associated with the re-advertised route.\" 071a80c5e5 and 690c96d6bf changed writeUpdateBody only to note AIGP recovery from the unfiltered withdrawal; the re-advertisement identifier generation (forward_path_id.go, forward_body.go, forward_provenance.go) is byte-identical, so the speaker's own Path Identifier on a re-advertised route stays proven. Verdict unchanged: enforced. Earlier judgement follows. Rejudged 2026-10-23 by an independent auditor (not the author) after 010e649057, which makes the final writer (session_ownership.go adjOutWrite.allow) send a synthesized withdrawal only to a destination holding an adj-out record for the route. RFC 4271 Section 3.1 allows a withdrawal only for \"the IP prefix that expresses the destination for a previously advertised route\". RFC 7911 Section 2: \"A BGP speaker that re-advertises a route MUST generate its own Path Identifier to be associated with the re-advertised route.\" The tagged carriers are byte-identical; what moved are two cited producers. session_ownership.go::allow: for a forwarded withdrawal of a key with no adj-out record it now returns false instead of owner.synthesized, so neither a received nor a synthesized withdrawal reaches a recipient that was never advertised the path; bindSourcePath still runs first and the refusal releases its binding as before. forward_provenance.go::prepareFwdProvenance: the per-path synthesized flag is removed and only the item-level synthesizedWithdrawal is kept. Neither change touches path-identifier minting, lookup, rewrite or release: generatePath, mintLocked, bindSourcePath, fwdPatchPathIDs and the writer patch are unchanged, and a withdrawal still carries the identifier the recipient was announced. The change removes the one case where a withdrawal could name a (prefix, Path Identifier) never advertised to that neighbor, which agrees with Section 5: \"If a BGP speaker receives a message to withdraw a prefix with a Path Identifier not seen before, it SHOULD silently ignore it.\" Ran on 2026-10-23: all TestForwardPathID* units, including TestForwardPathIDWithdrawOfUnknownPathLeavesNothing and TestForwardPathIDWithdrawCarriesTheAnnouncedValue, PASS. Prior judgement stands: carriers cover received identifiers zero and maximum, occupied wrap candidates, unframed ingress, independent destinations, IPv4 same/cross-context conversion, IPv6 MP_REACH/MP_UNREACH conversions, churn through reactorForwardRS with cache eviction, rebuilt reflection frames and mixed withdraw/reannounce, the FRR interop checker (two source-attributed paths with distinct identifiers) and the functional replay carrier. No numerical inequality from ingress, global uniqueness across prefixes or allocator reclamation policy is claimed.",
